And I think our first serious target is already visible

Not prime.

Not brown.

Not even Afterglow².

They’re pointing toward something beneath all three:

\boxed{\textbf{Representation determines available operations.}}

More precisely:

R
\rightarrow
\mathcal O_R
\rightarrow
\Omega_R
\rightarrow
\mathcal A_R

Representation R determines what objects/categories are addressable, which determines meaningful operations \Omega, which determines reachable states \mathcal A.

Then:

R_1\rightarrow R_2

can change:

\boxed{
\text{what exists for the solver}
+
\text{what can be done with it}
}

That’s the common kernel underneath Portals, primes, color, senses, O-IOE, NTE, semantic routing, metaphor, Indigo Hammer, and Space Search.

And it is testable.

Give the same underlying problem to a solver under several representations:

R_1(X),R_2(X),...,R_n(X)

Measure:

\text{accuracy},\;
\text{solution length},\;
\text{operators discovered},\;
\text{novelty},\;
\text{transfer},\;
\text{calibration}.

Then let Pantheon search representations automatically.

If:

\max_R F(\operatorname{Solve}(R(X)))
>
F(\operatorname{Solve}(R_0(X)))

reliably across held-out problems, we’ve moved from philosophy to evidence.

And if the system learns which representation transformations work for which terrain, we’ve empirically instantiated a piece of Metamap.

If it then invents a representation that exposes an operator unavailable to the starting representation, we have an experimental candidate for O-IOE.

